Adding a project = add `projects/<project>/chart` **and** a `projects/<project>/deploy.yaml`
listing only the envs you want deployed. Valid env symbols are exactly `dev` and `prod`; the two
may be **asymmetric**. Also add it to the root `README.md` project table —
`asgard-cli check` cross-checks that table against the actual folders.

## Entry point shapes — the field-level difference

**Which shape to use is decided by the audience** - see the three decisions
above. This table is what each one looks like once chosen.

| | **A. Agent hub** | **B. Self-hosted workflow, tools inline** | **C. Flow Agent** |
|---|---|---|---|
| Entry | the platform's per-namespace `preset-agent-hub` — you do not author it | your own `BotProvider` → your own `Workflow` | your own `BotProvider` → your own `Workflow` |
| Capabilities bound via | an `Agent` CR; the caller passes `agent_hub.agent_names[]` per turn | `toolsets` / `semanticLayers` written straight onto the `stream-llm-completion-message` processor | `sandboxBlueprint` on that processor → `SandboxBlueprint`'s own `toolsetNames` / `skillSetNames` / `sourceSetMounts` |
| Who can reach it | only a caller that can authenticate | anyone the BotProvider lets in | anyone the BotProvider lets in |
| `Agent` CRs in the chart | one per source system | none | none, unless a blueprint attaches subagents |

Field shapes to keep straight in shape C, all `ValueExprTemplate`: the names
fields carry a **comma-separated string** in `value`; `sourceSetMounts` carries a
**JSON string** the controller unmarshals (`sourceSetName` / optional `subPath` /
`mountPath` starting with `/` / `readOnly`). The workflow's processor **must not
re-declare `toolsets`** — that would be a second declaration of the same
capability, and the two would drift.

## SemanticLayer conventions

Common analysis views go in top-level `sampleQueries[]` (`comment` + `sql`),
**never** as a cube-level `sql:` virtual cube.

**Run every `sampleQuery` against the live database before committing it.** One
that errors or returns nonsense actively misleads the agent that reads it.

**A dimension's `name` is the column its `sql` selects** — `name: ProductId` with
`sql: '{CUBE}.ProductId'`, never a re-cased alias. Measure names are aggregates
rather than columns, so they stay descriptive.

**`description` on every cube, dimension and measure, in 繁體中文.** It is what
the agent reads to decide which column answers a question; one without a
description is effectively invisible to the model.


## This repo is a knowledge base, and you maintain it

Most of what is here is not code. It is what this engagement learned about one
customer's systems, and **this repository is the only place it exists** — the next
agent to open it starts from these files and nothing else. A pile of accurate
documents nobody can navigate is worth about what no documents are worth.

Four layers, and **the tense is the only reason they are separate**:

| layer | tense | answers |
|---|---|---|
| `docs/meeting-notes/` | a moment before it converged | what was actually discussed |
| `docs/decisions/` | a dated snapshot, **immutable** | why it was decided that way |
| `docs/spec/<<.SpecSlug>>/` | **always now** | what the system does today |
| `requirements/` | the future, then dead | what will be done |

> **If you read one thing to learn what this system does, read the living spec.**
> `requirements/` is a work queue, not a source of truth: a task spec stops being
> read the moment it reaches `done`, so anything durable left inside one is lost
> in place — still on disk, never opened again.

Three rules follow, and they are the whole discipline:

- **One fact, one home; everywhere else links.** About to copy a paragraph? Link
  instead. Two copies drift, and once they disagree the reader cannot tell which
  is current — so the second copy is worse than none, because it is believed.
- **A change is not done until the bookkeeping is done.** Index updated,
  cross-reference fixed, delta applied to the living spec. That half is yours:
  you do not get bored and can touch fifteen files in one pass. Do it in the
  same pass, rather than reporting that an index needs updating.
- **An answer you had to assemble is new knowledge.** If you answered a question
  by reading four files, the next reader will read the same four unless you file
  it. Ask: *would they have to redo this?*

`asgard-cli check` audits the mechanical half — pages nothing links to, a module
missing from its index, undated filenames, links that do not resolve. The half
it cannot see (pages that contradict each other, claims a newer source
superseded, concepts nobody owns) is the **`knowledge-base`** skill under
`.agents/skills/`. Run that pass when picking up a repo somebody else worked on,
before a handover, or after a burst of decisions.

Where a fact belongs when it is not obvious:
[`requirements/README.md`](requirements/README.md) for `requirements/`,
[`docs/README.md`](docs/README.md) for the layers,
[`references/README.md`](references/README.md) for the customer's own material.


## Two kinds of skills — design time vs runtime

They are **not interchangeable**. Deciding which one you are writing is the first question,
because the delivery mechanism is completely different.

| | `.agents/skills/<skill>/SKILL.md` | `common/skills/<skill>/SKILL.md` |
|---|---|---|
| **Who reads it** | the **coding agent** working in this repo (you) | the **deployed Asgard agent**, at runtime |
| **When** | design time — while authoring CRs | runtime — while answering a user |
| **How it is delivered** | read straight from the working tree | committed → `SourceSet` syncs the repo → `SkillSet` `searchPaths` selects it → bound by `Agent.managed.skillSetNames` or `SandboxBlueprint.spec.skillSetNames` |
| **Reaches the cluster?** | never | yes, that is the point |

**A SkillSet is a trio, not a CR.** The platform's `POST /v1/skill-set/from-git` creates a
`SkillSet` + its **own** `SourceSet` + the git `Syncer` that fills it, bound 1:1:1. Several
SkillSets sharing one SourceSet leaves the UI unable to find "this skill set's git config", and it
renders as a skill set with no source. Keep one file per SkillSet under
`templates/skill_set/<name>.yaml` holding all three CRs separated by `---`.

Three conventions, and what each one breaks:

| convention | why |
|---|---|
| the SourceSet's only member is **`git/`**, trailing slash included | the reconciler keys directory- vs file-member behaviour off that slash — without it the whole clone is treated as a single file |
| **`asgard-ai.com/managed-by: skill-set`** on the SourceSet *and* the Syncer | it hides the Syncer from the generic list, and it is what the platform cascades on when a SkillSet is deleted |
| a `searchPath` is **one skill directory** | one searchPath resolves to one skill; naming a parent directory silently resolves to nothing. `git/skills/pdf/`, not `git/skills/` |

## Display Annotations

Every CR the Platform UI lists needs an `asgard-ai.com/<kind>-name` annotation, in 繁體中文 —
without it the CR applies cleanly and then appears **nameless** in the UI, which no amount of
linting or dry-running reveals. `asgard-cli verify` enforces it.

| kind | required | also useful |
|---|---|---|
| `Agent` | `agent-name` | — (`spec.managed.description` already carries the description) |
| `SemanticLayer` | `semantic-layer-name` | — |
| `DataConnector` | `data-connector-name` | — |
| `SkillSet` | `skill-set-name` | `skill-set-description` |
| `SourceSet` | `source-set-name` | `source-set-description` |
| `Syncer` | `syncer-name` | `syncer-description` |
| `BotProvider` | `bot-provider-name` | `additional-annotation` → `embedConfig`(widget 外觀) |
| `SandboxBlueprint` | `sandbox-blueprint-name` | `sandbox-blueprint-description` |
| `Toolset` | `toolset-name` | `toolset-description` |
| `Workflow` | `workflow-name` | — |
| `Trigger` | `trigger-name` | — |

> **The authority for every platform key is `asgard-ai-platform/asgard-workflow-service`,
> `internal/shared.go`** — every `Annotation*` / `Label*` constant the platform reads, with
> comments on what it does. Read it rather than inferring a convention from another repo's charts.
> **Anything not on `asgard-cli verify`'s list is invisible to every check here**, so a new UI
> label is a check to add, not just a line to write.

## Workflow sets — the metadata that decides whether the UI shows anything

**A `Workflow` CR is not the platform's UI unit; a *workflow set* is** — the group of Workflows
sharing `asgard-ai.com/workflow-set-id`, exactly one of which carries `main-workflow-set: "true"`.
Everything here is UI-only: **nothing changes runtime**, and a CR omitting all of it is perfectly
valid, so `helm lint`, CRD validation and server dry-run all stay green.

| what's missing | what the user sees |
|---|---|
| the set labels | the Workflow is in no set → nothing to list. An empty Flow Agents / Automations page |
| annotation `workflow-set-name` | the set is listed, nameless |
| the Trigger CR's own two labels | the Trigger editor opens as a blank canvas |

| on the Workflow | value | what it decides |
|---|---|---|
| label `workflow-set-id` | the CR name (one workflow = one set is the simple case) | which set this Workflow belongs to |
| label `main-workflow-set` | `"true"` | which member is the set's entry — exactly one. Zero → never listed; two → the front end picks whichever came first |
| label `workflow-key` | the CR name | the Workflow's own identity inside the set |
| label `workflow-set-type` | `bot` / `automation_tool` / `trigger` | which UI section it appears in |
| annotation `workflow-set-name` | 顯示名 | the **set's** name in the UI (`workflow-name` is the single Workflow's) |

**There are three set types, not two.** `trigger` was added later, so a CR dumped from an older
cluster shows `automation_tool` for a Trigger workflow and **is not a template to copy**.

**A `Trigger` needs two labels of its own**, invisible from the Workflow side: its editor reads
`asgard-ai.com/workflow-set-id` (equal to its entrypoint Workflow's) and
`asgard-ai.com/project-environment-id` straight off the Trigger CR. The platform copies both onto
a Trigger it creates itself; **a hand-written CR has nobody to copy them for it.**

**Write only Workflow + Trigger.** The `Trigger` reconciler provisions the BotProvider, its
API-key Secret, and the CronJob itself. Hand-writing a BotProvider for a Trigger is wrong.

## `platformMainEnvironmentId`

Each project's K8s Namespace maps to a Platform Project whose main environment has an ID. It is
**per-project per-env** → set it in each project's `values-<env>.yaml` (default `""` in
`values.yaml`, never hardcoded in templates). It surfaces as the label
`asgard-ai.com/project-environment-id`, carried by **`Workflow`, `CompletionModel` and `Trigger`**
only.

**You cannot get this ID until the namespace exists** and the platform has reconciled it into a
Project — which is why Terraform comes first (see Deploy / CI).

## Completion Model

**Default: use the platform's builtins by name.** No CR, no model API key in `app-secret`. One
knob — `defaultCompletionModelName`, read by every `SemanticLayer` and overridable per-env.
**Never hardcode a model name in a template**: the builtin tiers are semantic aliases, and a
specific name becomes something to come back and fix when that model is retired.

**A `CompletionModel` CR exists and is the exception, not an impossibility.** Write one when the
customer brings their own model or their own key — three reference deployments do:

    completionModelClass:  aoai-chat | openai-chat | gemini | anthropic | mistral | builtin
    exactly one of:        aoaiChat, openaiChat, gemini, anthropic, mistral, builtin
    the key:               spec.<provider>.apiKey.valueFrom.secretKeyRef into app-secret

Two things the CRD enforces and helm will not tell you: **`completionModelClass` is immutable** —
changing it means a new CR, not an edit — and exactly one provider block may be present, so a
half-migrated CR with two is rejected by the apiserver, not by `helm lint`.

It also carries `asgard-ai.com/project-environment-id`, like `Workflow` and `Trigger`.

`SemanticLayer.spec.completionModelName` is **required**; an `Agent` takes no model (the caller
picks the orchestrator's model per turn).

## Secrets

All secrets are read from the Kubernetes secret `app-secret` (configurable via `values.yaml` →
`appSecretName`), provisioned per project by infra (Terraform), **not by this repo**.

- `asgard_resource_api_key` — the platform resource key. **One key per namespace, shared by every
  CR that needs one**: `SourceSet.apiKey`, `Toolset.apiKey` and `BotProvider.adminApiKey` are all
  platform resource credentials of the same kind. Do **not** mint a per-CR key for them.
- `asgard-github-pat-password` — only needed when a git `Syncer` clones a **private** repo.
- one `<system>_db_password` per database.

Charts **only ever write `secretKeyRef`**; credential values never enter values files or git.

## Deploy / CI

CI/CD is tag-driven via `.github/workflows/main.yaml`. A single tag rolls out every project **that
declares this env** in its `deploy.yaml`; projects without that env are skipped. A `setup` job
resolves env/cluster from the tag and scans every `projects/*/deploy.yaml` into a matrix; a
`deploy` job runs **one parallel job per matched project**, does `helm upgrade`, then **triggers
that project's Syncers once and waits for them to complete**.

| Tag | Env |
|---|---|
| `dev-x.y.z` | dev |
| `x.y.z` | prod |

Namespaces follow `asgard-<<.Workspace.Slug>>-<project>-<env>`. The project slug is deliberately
short so that names derived from the namespace stay short.

> **Two ordering traps, both of which fail in CD rather than locally:**
>
> 1. **Terraform first.** Enabling an env for a project means: create that env's namespace and
>    `app-secret` in tf-asgard **first**, then add `environments.<env>` to its `deploy.yaml` and the
>    matching `chart/values-<env>.yaml`. In the other order the next tag just fails.
> 2. **CD requires at least one `Syncer` per deployed project.** The "trigger and wait" step polls
>    for CronJobs labelled `asgard-ai.com/syncer-name` and **exits 1 after 180s if it finds none**,
>    even when `helm upgrade` succeeded.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Deployment is CD-only — do not `helm upgrade` from a laptop.** A Syncer that pins
> `revision: {{ .Chart.AppVersion }}` needs the release tag CI stamps into `appVersion`; a local
> install renders the placeholder version as a git ref that does not exist, and the Syncer fails to
> clone every run. `asgard-cli render` therefore renders only; it has no install path.

Required repo secrets: `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` / `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` / `AWS_REGION`.

## Helm Commands

```bash
# Lint a project chart
helm lint ./projects/<project>/chart/app -f ./projects/<project>/chart/values-dev.yaml

# Render locally — prefer the helper: it takes namespace/values from deploy.yaml, so the
# output is exactly what CD deploys (same overlay order, --namespace and release name)
asgard-cli render <project> dev
```

Release name is the project name, in both CI and locally. `asgard-cli render` needs only `helm`.

## Acceptance Gate

Load the **`asgard-cr-verification`** skill (`.agents/skills/`) and run its gate; project omitted =
whole repo. **Any red step stops the work.**

1. `asgard-cli check [project]` — structural invariants
2. `helm lint projects/<project>/chart/app` (bare — proves `values.yaml` defaults every
   `.Values.*`) and again with `-f projects/<project>/chart/values-dev.yaml`
3. `asgard-cli verify [project]` — renders, then checks CR cross-reference completeness
   **and** the one-system-one-Agent invariants. One command, both sets.
4. **Against the cluster you deploy to** (read-only, needs `kubectl`):
   `kubectl apply --dry-run=server` **and** `python3 scripts/check_crd_fidelity.py - --context <ctx>`

Step 4 is two checks because they catch different things: dry-run answers "will it be accepted",
fidelity answers "**will it be kept**". See the pruning gotcha below.

## Spec-Driven Development

- Treat `requirements/` as implementation source of truth; `references/` is background only.
- Task specs: `requirements/tasks/TASK-xxx-*.md`, registered in `requirements/tasks/_index.md`.
- Status flow: `draft` → `ready` → `in-progress` → `done` (never `in_progress`).
- **What may live in `requirements/` at all is ruled by
  [`requirements/README.md`](requirements/README.md)** — two record types and
  nothing else, and the tense test that decides the rest: `requirements/` is
  written in the future tense, the living spec in the present. A sentence still
  true after the work is finished belongs in the living spec, not in a task spec
  that stops being read at `done`. Read it before putting anything in
  `requirements/` that is not a `REQ-*.md` or a `TASK-*.md`.
- SDD is **required** for a new `SemanticLayer`/`DataConnector`, widening an agent's queryable
  cubes, or re-introducing a write path — the databases are real.

**This file and the living spec answer different questions, and a fact belongs to exactly one:**

| | `AGENTS.md` (here) | `docs/spec/<<.SpecSlug>>/` |
|---|---|---|
| Answers | **how to change this repo** | **what the system does** |
| Holds | layout, template conventions, Helm/CRD gotchas, how to run the gate | capability boundaries, data 口徑, routing rules, state machines, the security argument |
| Test | "not knowing this breaks the chart" | "not knowing this makes the behaviour wrong" |

**When a change lands**, two obligations beyond the code: apply the behaviour delta to the matching
`docs/spec/<<.SpecSlug>>/<module>.md`, and write one `docs/decisions/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>.md` for
each decision that got settled.

## Helm / Platform Gotchas

- All resources use `apiVersion: asgard-ai.com/v1alpha1`.
- Keep dev/prod differences in `projects/<project>/chart/values-*.yaml` and `common/values-*.yaml`
  only.
- **A field the CRD does not declare is silently pruned, and `kubectl apply --dry-run=server` calls
  that success.** Helm's server-side apply does not — it builds a typed patch and fails with
  `field not declared in schema`, so the first sign is a red CD run.
  `scripts/check_crd_fidelity.py` exists for exactly this.
- **Read CRD shapes from the cluster you deploy to**, not from a local checkout of the CRD repo —
  the two can disagree, and a deprecated field is easy to find in an older checkout.
- **`Workflow.spec.processors[].configs[].name` is a free-form string.** A wrong config name lints
  clean, passes CRD validation, and then does nothing at runtime. A typo in a *reference* is
  caught by `asgard-cli verify`; an invented *key* is not.
- **A failed `helm upgrade` can leave the namespace partially applied.** The previous revision
  stays `deployed`, so the next upgrade proceeds without a rollback — but do not assume "the deploy
  failed" means "nothing changed".
- **A resource the platform created cannot be taken over by helm without a one-time adoption.**
  Helm refuses with `invalid ownership metadata` unless the live object already carries
  `app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: Helm`.
- `Toolset.tools[]` require `requestConsent` (bool); `Agent.managed.prompt` requires all of
  `persona`/`task`/`context`/`format`, and `managed.aliasName` + `managed.description` are required.
- Render with `asgard-cli render` before committing template changes; there is no app to run.
